# Kotlin style & conventions

**Primary source:** the [Android Kotlin style
guide](https://developer.android.com/kotlin/style-guide) and the
[Kotlin official coding
conventions](https://kotlinlang.org/docs/coding-conventions.html) —
Google's and JetBrains' own guidance respectively. These are followed as
if repeated here in their entirety; this file does not restate them, it
points to them as the actual source of truth.

**Community layer:** ktlint (implements the official Kotlin style guide
directly — its stated philosophy is "there should be only one way to
format code," making it more of a direct enforcement tool than a
competing opinion) + detekt (static analysis rules beyond formatting —
complexity, potential bugs) as the **automated enforcement layer only**.

## Enforcement, not debate

- `.editorconfig` (ktlint reads this) and `detekt.yml` in the foundation
  scaffold encode the official style guide, unmodified unless a specific
  rule conflicts with a documented project need — and that conflict gets
  a one-line comment in the config explaining why, not a silent removal.
- Format-on-save (ktlint) resolves style debates in PR review before they
  happen — reviewers comment on logic and naming clarity, not brace
  placement.
- CI gate 1/2 (`testing-verify-flows.mdc`) runs ktlint + detekt with zero
  tolerance for new violations; existing violations in code the PR
  doesn't touch are not blocking.

## Naming (Android Kotlin style guide highlights relevant to app code)

- `UpperCamelCase` for classes/objects/interfaces, `lowerCamelCase` for
  functions/properties/parameters, `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` only for
  top-level or companion-object `const val`s.
- Name booleans as assertions: `isLoading`, `hasError` — never `loading`,
  `error` (ambiguous whether it's the boolean or the value) — same
  convention as `ios-swift-rules`' naming guidance.
- Acronyms in names follow standard camel case (`userId`, `apiUrl`),
  matching the Kotlin convention (unlike Swift's all-caps acronym rule) —
  do not import Swift naming habits into Kotlin code.

## Kotlin idioms adopted alongside the official style guide

- Prefer `val` over `var`; prefer immutable collections
  (`List`, `Map`) over mutable ones at API boundaries.
- Prefer expression bodies and `when` over imperative `if`/`else` chains
  where it improves readability — but do not force single-expression
  functions where a multi-statement body is clearer.
- Use data classes for simple value holders (DTOs, `UiState`); use sealed
  interfaces/classes for closed sets of alternatives (`ApiError`,
  `Intent`).
- Use dedicated logging (`AppLogger`/Timber per `logging-observability.mdc`)
  instead of `println()`/raw `Log.d` calls scattered through application
  code.

